<BaseLayout title={'FAQ: working with The Aha Company'} description={'How an engagement with The Aha Company runs, for institutions putting regulated assets onchain, protocols expanding to new networks and ecosystems.'}>
<main class="faq_main"><div class="intro"><p class="v-eyebrow page_eyebrow">FAQ</p><h1 class="intro_h1">Questions we hear before the first call.</h1><p class="text-lead">Answered from the engagements we have run. If yours is not here, ask it on the call.</p></div><div class="faq_layout"><nav aria-label="FAQ sections" class="faq_subnav"><a class="faq_pill" href="#working">Working with us</a><a class="faq_pill" href="#institutions">Financial institutions</a><a class="faq_pill" href="#protocols">Protocols and products</a><a class="faq_pill" href="#ecosystems">Ecosystems and foundations</a></nav><div class="faq_content"><section class="faq_group" id="working"><h2 class="faq_group-h2">Working with us</h2><div class="faq_list"><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What does The Aha Company do?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">We are a blockchain integration partner. Three kinds of clients come to us: financial institutions putting regulated assets onchain, protocols and products expanding to a new network, and ecosystems and foundations that need developer tooling. The engineers who build the networks' tooling do the integrations.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">How does an engagement start?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">With a working session, not a pitch. For an institution, a scoping session with your technical lead; for a protocol, an integration roadmap; for an ecosystem, a two-week DevX audit. Then an NDA and an MOU with the scope cut into milestones your team can verify.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">How long does it take?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-duration">The first two weeks go to scoping: a scoping session, an integration roadmap or a DevX audit, depending on your case. Delivery then runs in milestones your team can verify. The <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/nda-settlement-stablecoin">internal settlement stablecoin of a top US commercial bank</a> took five months from kickoff to handover, in 2026.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What are the ways to work with you?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Four. Integration partner: fixed scope, milestone-based, our team end to end. Architecture lead: we design the system and lead delivery with your engineers. Embedded principal engineer: a principal engineer placed with your team for a quarter or more. Programs for ecosystems: DevX audits, tooling roadmaps, accelerator and hackathon mentoring.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Who will we be talking to?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">An engineer. The first call is thirty minutes with someone who has shipped the kind of system you are asking about, not a sales team.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Where is the team based?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-team">Engineers across the United States, France and Brazil. The company is Dawning Inc. (dba The Aha Company), incorporated in Delaware.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Which networks do you work on?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Stellar, where we are core contributors, Stellar Community Fund reviewers and tooling maintainers. XRPL, as a Ripple program partner. Canton, where integrations are in progress. Ethereum as the EVM counterpart for cross-chain work.</p></details></div></section><section class="faq_group" id="institutions"><h2 class="faq_group-h2">Financial institutions</h2><div class="faq_list"><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Have you taken regulated assets to mainnet?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-mainnet">Yes. <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/societe-generale-forge">EURCV</a>, Société Générale-FORGE's regulated euro stablecoin, has been live on Stellar mainnet since Q1 2026, with €20M+ issued: we built its issuance and governance. We also architected <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/nda-settlement-stablecoin">an internal settlement stablecoin for a top US commercial bank</a>, live on Stellar mainnet. In build or in design: <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/nda-money-market-fund">a tokenized share class of a money market fund</a> for a regulated European asset manager, and <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/nda-real-estate-debt-fund">a real estate fund issued natively on Stellar</a> for a European sponsor.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Are the contracts audited, and by whom?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-audits">Client contracts, yes, before mainnet: Hacken audited the launch contracts of <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/societe-generale-forge">EURCV</a> on Soroban, and Halborn the <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/lifi">LI.FI</a> contracts on Stellar. Among our open-source tools, Veridise audited the <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/stellar-registry">Stellar Registry</a> (no findings) and Runtime Verification the core contracts of <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/moonlight">Moonlight</a>; Nido stays in a curated release until its external audit, and Tansu, Perch and GhostSig have not had one yet. Each page says where it stands.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What compliance controls can you put in place?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">On the asset, where the network provides them: holder authorization, freeze and clawback, which is how EURCV’s compliance runs on Stellar. In contracts, where the rules go further: role separation between admin, mint and burn authority, allow and deny lists, transfer restrictions for fund units, subscriptions and redemptions onchain. Around them: alerting-to-freeze automation and mint and burn bridges to your core banking.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">How do you handle custody and keys?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Custodian and MPC integrations, KYT through the custodian, smart accounts and passkeys. Who holds which key is settled in the scoping session, before any code is written.</p></details><details class="faq_item" data-rv="faq-privacy"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Can payments stay private on a public ledger?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Yes: private, not anonymous. <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/moonlight">Moonlight</a>, our open-source privacy layer for Stellar payments, lets a regulated provider know each customer, while the ledger shows amounts and flows between one-time keys, but no names. Its core contracts were audited by Runtime Verification, and it is in a limited mainnet release for invite-only pilots.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What happens after mainnet?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Production surfaces questions a testnet never asks. Deployment comes with runbooks and handover, and we keep evolving the system with your team after launch.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Can you work inside our team?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-pwc">Yes. In the architecture-lead model we design the system and lead delivery with your engineers. A principal engineer can also join your team for a quarter or more.</p></details></div></section><section class="faq_group" id="protocols"><h2 class="faq_group-h2">Protocols and products</h2><div class="faq_list"><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What does integrating onto a new network involve?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">An integration roadmap that maps your surface to the target network, then native contracts, adapters and SDKs, routing and liquidity, matched to the guarantees you already give users and tested against your existing suite.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Have you done this before?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Yes. <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/lifi">LI.FI's Stellar integration</a> runs on Soroban contracts we built, extending its cross-chain reach.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Can the integration be funded?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Most integrations run on the client's own budget. Ecosystem programs fund some of them: when one fits, we prepare the application with you, and we say early when none applies.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Who maintains the integration after launch?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Your engineers can. Launch comes with docs and a maintenance plan written for your team to run.</p></details></div></section><section class="faq_group" id="ecosystems"><h2 class="faq_group-h2">Ecosystems and foundations</h2><div class="faq_list"><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What is a DevX audit?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Two senior engineers spend two weeks on your real onboarding path, then deliver a tooling roadmap agreed with your engineering team: CLIs, scaffolding, registries, sandboxes, wallets.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">What have you built for ecosystems?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-ecosystems">For Stellar: core contributions to the <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/stellar-cli">Stellar CLI</a>; <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/stellar-scaffold">Stellar Scaffold</a> and the <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/stellar-registry">Stellar Registry</a>; <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/nido">Nido</a>, our open-source passkey wallet, and <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/perch">Perch</a>, its policy layer; <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/moonlight">Moonlight</a>, a privacy layer for payments; and <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/tansu">Tansu</a>, onchain governance and release records. For XRPL: <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source/ghostsig">GhostSig</a>, a passkey wallet for XRPL and Stellar, and mentoring in <a class="faq_answer-link" href="/case-studies/ripple">Ripple’s accelerator cohorts and hackathons</a>, from prototype to go live.</p></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Is the tooling open source?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><div class="faq_answer" data-rv="faq-os"><div class="inline-text">Yes. Stellar Scaffold, the Stellar Registry and Tansu are maintained as public goods, with a quarterly proposal and review. Each tool has its own page, with where its code lives, its status, its audits and who uses it: see our </div><a class="faq_answer-link" href="/open-source">open-source page</a><div class="inline-text">.</div></div></details><details class="faq_item"><summary class="faq_q"><div class="inline-text">Can you place an engineer with our team?</div><span aria-hidden="true" class="faq_icon">+</span></summary><p class="faq_answer">Yes, a principal engineer for a quarter or more, as with the Stellar Development Foundation.</p></details></div></section></div></div></main>
<Cta heading={'Still have a question? Ask an engineer.'} />
</BaseLayout>
